<div class="eyebrow">GUIDE / COLLABORATION</div><h1>Planning remote meetings across time zones</h1><p class="lead">Start with each participant’s local date, not just a list of UTC offsets.</p><p class="notice">WorldScope editorial team · Updated November 2026 · <a href="/info/methodology/">Methodology</a></p><h2>Find the overlap</h2><p>List the locations and date, then compare local working hours for each participant. For teams across Africa and Europe, remember that daylight saving rules differ by country and may change the overlap during the year. Public holidays, work patterns and weekends also vary.</p><h2>When no slot is ideal</h2><p>Rotate inconvenient times instead of always asking the same people to join late or early. Consider asynchronous updates, record decisions in writing, and ask participants to confirm their availability.</p><h2>Use the meeting finder</h2><p>Add your cities to the dashboard and select a reference date. The finder ranks one-hour slots by how many locations fall within weekday 09:00–17:00 local time. It does not check holidays, individual availability or calendars, so verify before sending an invitation.</p><p><a class="button" href="/#planning">Find meeting times ↗</a></p>
